#![allow(clippy::excessive_precision)]
//! Golden-parity test for simulate_weather (PARITY_TESTING.md).
//! Reference HTML lines 5299-5719 (circulationCells/buildWind/simulateWeather/
//! blurCoarse/bilC/sampleArr/satCap).
//!
//! Deferred, matching this port's established pattern -- documented, not silently
//! dropped (see cartalith-native/docs/CHANGELOG.md):
//!   - ocean-current SST folding (oceanSSTAnomaly / state.climate.currents)
//!   - terrain wind deflection (deflectFlow, buildWind's opts.elev branch)
//!   - world-structure continental-interior dryness
//!
//! MVP_SCOPE.md explicitly names ocean-current terrain coupling a stretch goal;
//! the other two are deferred under the same reasoning, tracked explicitly here.
//!
//! **Tolerance, not exact equality** -- the one exception among this port's
//! golden tests so far, and a deliberate one (PARITY_TESTING.md: "give each
//! field its own tolerance and record the reasoning"). `build_wind` and the
//! advection loop both call `.hypot()`, and `Math.hypot` is explicitly listed
//! in ECMA-262 among the "implementation-approximated" Math functions --
//! unlike `+`/`-`/`*`/`/`, engines are not required to produce bit-identical
//! results for it. Bisected by comparing exact f32 bit patterns between a
//! Node run and a Rust debug build: `wx`/`wy` differ from JS by exactly 1 ULP
//! at a handful of cells immediately after `build_wind` returns, before the
//! iteration loop even starts -- confirming the divergence is real,
//! understood, and not a translation mistake compounding over iterations.
//! `1e-5` absolute tolerance is generous relative to the observed 1-ULP
//! (~1e-7) drift, while still catching a genuine formula error.

use cartalith_climate::{simulate_weather, WeatherParams};

/// `PARITY_TESTING.md`'s tolerance discipline applied to `simulate_weather`
/// specifically -- see the module doc comment for why this one function
/// (unlike every other golden test in this port) isn't held to exact
/// equality.
fn assert_close(actual: &[f32], expected: &[f32]) {
    const TOL: f32 = 1e-5;
    for (i, (&a, &e)) in actual.iter().zip(expected.iter()).enumerate() {
        assert!(
            (a - e).abs() <= TOL,
            "index {i}: got {a}, expected {e} (diff {})",
            (a - e).abs()
        );
    }
}

